ntly how we could get money  invested in the green recovery and i  think that's a hugely important issue  i'm from ireland but at the college i  represent all of europe not just ireland  how does a commissioner decide which two  laws to propose  so essentially the college of  commissioners draft legislation or  indeed change existing legislation but  we don't do it on our own we listen to  stakeholders those who have an interest  in that policy area and sometimes we ask  people to give us their views of what's  coming up  how can ordinary people have a say into  proposed or existing eu laws  citizens businesses and organizations  can get involved in europe's future  direction by sharing their views on new  eu laws or on how to improve existing  laws via public consultations process on  the commission website  you can log on and give your views on  specific issues that are open for public  consultations and a very important  public consultation underway at the  moment is the conference on the future  of europe we really would welcome young  people's views as to how the european  union should look in the future  and how does a policy proposal become an  actual eu law  once a policy proposal is approved by  the 27 commissioners it then moves  forward to the council of the european  union  and to the european parliament and both  those european institutions then decide  whether it will become a law or not  the council of the european union is  made up of government ministers from the  different eu countries and depending on  the policy proposal you will see the  relevant ministers attending  the european parliament is made up of  705 members from the 27 eu member states  how often are members of the european  parliament elected  meps are directly elected by eu voters  every five years to promote the  interests of the 450 million citizens  the allocation of mep seats is based  purely on population size and the more  the country grows the more possibility  they'll be able