en deal  the eu was leading the way in dealing  ambitiously with two of the biggest  challenges that the global community  faces the eu is proud to be the world's  biggest donor of overseas development  aid to poorer more vulnerable countries  and as a result of the eu being a global  economic superpower in worldwide trade  affairs irish exporters and consumers  benefit hugely by being a member of the  european union ireland has a bigger say  in big issues that affect both the eu  and global community  [Music]  so how exactly does the eu work  each of the 27 member countries of the  european union remains an independent  self-governing state  however  in certain areas member states delegate  or hand over some of their  decision-making powers to shared eu  institutions they have created  so if you member states make important  decisions about certain things  how do they do it and where are they  doing from  three key institutions in eu decision  making are the european commission the  council of the european union and the  european parliament  the european commission based in  brussels is responsible for the  day-to-day running of the eu it is  responsible for proposing policies and  new laws  and this is decided upon by a college of  27 commissioners one from each member  state  the european parliament approves the  president of the commission the current  president of the commission is ursula  van der line who is from germany and the  term of office is five years both for  the president and for the college of  commissioners  what responsibilities do individual  commissioners have and whose views do  they represent  commissioners do not represent their  countries instead they have a portfolio  of areas of responsibility given to them  by the commission president  in my case that's everything to do with  the financial system the stock market  and financial stability but i'm also  dealing with new things like digital  innovation in the financial area and  very importantly how we could get money  invested in the green recovery and i  think that's a hugely important issue  i'm from ireland but at the college i  represent all of europe not just ireland  how does a commissioner decide which two  laws to propose  so essentially the college of  commissioners draft legislation or  indeed change existing legislation but  we don't do it on our own we listen to  stakeholders those who have an interest  in that policy area and sometimes we ask  people to give us their views of what's  coming up  how can ordinary people have a say into  proposed or existing eu laws  citizens businesses and organizations  can get involved in europe's future  direction by sharing their views on new  eu laws or on how to improve existing  laws via public consultations process on  the commission website  you can log on and give your views on  specific issues that are open for public  consultations and a very important  public consultation underway at the  moment is the conference on the future  of europe we really would welcome young  people's views as to how the european  union should look in the future  and how does a policy proposal become an  actual eu law  once a policy proposal is approved by  the 27 commissioners it then moves  forward to the council of the european  union  and to the european parliament and both  those european institutions then decide  whether it will become a law or not  the council of the european union is  made up of government ministers from the  different eu countries and depending on  the policy proposal you will see the  relevant ministers attending  the european parliament is made up of  705 members from the 27 eu member states  how often are members of the european  parliament elected  meps are directly elected by eu voters  every five years to promote the  interests of the 450 million citizens  the allocation of mep seats is based  purely on population size and the more  the country grows the more possibility  they'll be able to add meps in ireland  we have 13 in total  once a month we vote in what we call  plenary so voting sessions and they are  done in strasbourg in france the other  three weeks of the month we have  meetings in brussels in belgium  does the european parliament and the  council of the european union always  agree on new laws  when the council and the parliament  don't agree on legislation which is  actually quite a lot we have to meet up  and agree a compromise before a new law  is enacted negotiations begin each site  outlining the important issues for them  and it's about concentrating on the  issues that unite us rather than divide  us  once a law is adopted it is the role of  the european commission to ensure that  it is properly applied in all member  states  what role does our tea shop have to  european union the thieshok and other eu  member states leaders all form part of  what is known as the european council  it holds regular summit meetings during  the year to discuss the overall  political direction of the eu  you could say they need to discuss the  big picture  okay i'm beginning to understand how it  all works but are there any more eu  institutions other important eu  institutions include the european  central bank the main bank of the 19  eurozone countries  the european court of justice ensures  all eu laws are applied correctly and  the european court of auditors oversees  the proper management of eu money  all of the eu institutions exist to  ensure that the european union operates  in an open democratic and transparent  way and that citizens interests and  rights are protected  [Music]  what about working for the eu are there  many opportunities for young people  each year around 1 900 young eu  graduates are offered the chance to  apply to take part in one of the eu  trainee programs  opportunities are available in a variety  of eu institutions and in a wide range  of fields including competition law  human resources environmental policy  communication and many more  these are periods of time where you get  your foot in the door you get a sense of  what the eu does how it works and you'll  get a sense of what kind of job you  would like to do within the eu  institutions as well i'm based in  brussels my job involves  all sorts of communication my speciality  is social media so i get to research  what works and what doesn't work on  social media  so i started as a trainee in the  communication unit traineeships are  important because you  need people to explain things to you  it's also important for making friends  and  meeting people who are at the same stage  of life that you're at at the same stage  of career that you're at to find out  more visit epso dot europa dot eu  forward slash job opportunities forward  slash traineeships  aside from traineeships what other  long-term career prospects does the  european union offer  the eu institutions employ over 40 000  people mainly in the european union but  also in locations all around the world  there are so many different career  options within the eu institutions  anything from being a lawyer to being an  accountant working on human resources  for any eu civil servant we all have to  have two eu languages when we start off  but to stay within the eu institutions  you'd need to have three eu languages at  your disposal i would for example have  started off with irish english i french  and i brought german with me as well  one of the very interesting jobs that  are available to people with languages  is the job of being an interpreter  working as an eu interpreter here in  brussels is a really exciting job you're  right at the center of the action and  all the debate that goes on within the  european union and you really do get an  opportunity to see history in the making  here in the european institutions for  example politicians citizens and  officials can speak any one of the eu's  24 official languages interpreters  translate a message from one language to  another aloud and in real time so that  everybody can understand and follow  what's being said  i interpret from french german and irish  into english  you've also got a range of  administrative jobs and that can be  anything from making sure that policies  are put together and are implemented to  helping the politicians communicate  their policies as well  i'm a communications manager at  eurofound located in dublin and our role  is to provide knowledge and information  to eu policymakers and national  governments and social partners to help  them shape better policies for citizens  whether it's gender issues industrial  relations employment and bigger issues  like climate change  i work in a multicultural environment i  work with people from lots of different  countries and it's great to be part of  the european project but at the same  time as an irish person i get to live in  ireland diversity is also one of the  reasons that i love working in the  european commission you get people from  all over europe but also all over the  world and  you're never short of an interesting  conversation  so if i had any advice for young people  i'd say brush up on your languages as an  irish person native english speaker you  have a huge advantage but if you can arm  yourself with french with spanish or  another language i think so many more  doors will be open to you  on the 1st of january 2022 irish will  become a full official working language  of the eu institutions we need  translators we need interpreters we need  proofreaders and we need lawyer  linguists if you'd like to work for the  eu institutions in the future take a  look at our website eu ireland dot ie we  have a page there work for the eu which  gives you a sense of the different  career opportunities available and the  places where you could find information  as a young person who wants to find out  even more about the eu and get involved  what can i do  there are many dynamic and interesting  ways young irish people can get involved  including online and in-person  participation  for almost 50 years ireland has been an  active and valued member of the eu we  have benefited hugely from our  involvement and we've contributed to its  success  for this generation and generations to  come ireland's future is a european  future  i'm proud to be a member of the eu  family  it's a very big family  there are  450 million of us in 27 member countries  and as an eu citizen i can travel freely  to any of these countries  i can shop without any extra tariff or  cost  for goods and services from any one of  the member states  and thanks to eu laws both me  and me have the right to equal pay for  equal work  and every eu citizen can work freely  in any one of the 27 member countries  to find out more follow the european  commission representation in ireland on  twitter instagram and facebook
 

